      {
        "finding_id": "section-d7b5cad43a7571554084",
        "file": "ournavybarbaryco00alle_ournavybarbaryco00alle_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Our navy and the Barbary corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/ournavybarbaryco00alle",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Allen, Gardner Weld, 1856-1944"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1905-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e15e3a557e79edec2ea71b2b5ccd1015a5ec882a666c35aba27115b05eadb074",
        "evidence_excerpt": "As  before  mentioned,  Mr.  Barlow  remained  at  Algiers \nduring  negotiations  with  Tunis  and  Tripoli.  He  se- \ncured the  services  at  Tunis  of  a  Frenchman  named \nJoseph  Famin,  who  had  been  recommended  to  him  by \nthe  French  consul.  Famin  concluded,  without  expense, \na  truce  for  six  months  between  the  United  States  and \nTunis,  beginning  June  15,  1796.^  In  a  letter  dated \nSeptember  1,  Barlow  mentions  the  capture  of  two \nAmerican  ships  by  the  Tripolitans,  one  of  which  turned \nout  to  be  the  Sophia  with  O'Brien  and  the  money  for \nthe  Algerine  treaty ;  the  other  ship,  he  says,  was \nbroken  up  and  the  cargo  confiscated.  These  vessels \nwere  taken  by  the  Tripolitan  corsair  Peter  Lisle,  a \nrenegade  Scotchman  known  as  Murad  Reis.  Octo- \nber 9  Barlow  speaks  of  the  capture  of  a  vessel  by \nthe  Tunisians,  apparently  in  violation  of  the  truce. \nOctober  12  he  writes :  \"  Another  large  ship,  richly \nladened,  the  Betsy  of  Boston,  Captain  Sampson,  has \nbeen  taken  at  Tripoli.  The  crew  are  in  slavery  ;  they \nwill  soon  be  free.\"  No  further  details  of  these  captures \nare  given.  Upon  the  conclusion  of  peace  with  Tripoli, \nwhich  soon  followed,  the  Betsy  was  retained  by  the \npasha,  but  her  crew  were  released.^",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1796
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
