        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "7. A useful term for the pirate enclaves—perhaps still not quite the \nmot juste — might be “ordered anarchy”, originally applied by E. E. \nEvans-Pritchard to the tribal organization of the Nuer, and quoted \nby Richard Drinnon, who re-applies it to the “red-white republic of \nFredonia” founded in Texas by the Cherokee chief Richard Fields \nand the fascinating John Dunn Hunter—a white who'd been cap- \ntured by Indians as an infant, went to London where he met Robert \nOwen and other radicals, and returned to America in 1824. Hunter \nwas another kind of Renegado—a convert to “Indianism”—and as \nsuch was hated and denounced. Fredonia failed and Hunter was",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1824
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
