      {
        "finding_id": "section-2c4216579a4fc451d424",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The city sent out two well-equipped gal- \nleys to do him honor. The officer corps of the \nDivan assembled in the number of five hun- \ndred to receive him at the port, where as he \ndisembarked from his galley he was received \nwith a salute of some fifteen hundred guns \nfrom the city forts and the corsair ships some \nforty of which came out under sail. There then \nmarched the Agha of the Janissaries accompa- \nnied by two drummers (Cavus), followed by \nthe Principal Secretary with the 24 Ayabashis \nwho are the chief Counselors of State. There \nfollowed two by two the Bulukbashis with \ntheir huge plumed turbans, then the ranks of \nthe Odabashis; there marched after them six \nTurkish oboists with Moors among them some \nplaying flutes and other cymbals, the whole \nensemble a very strange noise which aroused \nin us more fear than pleasure. Last came the \nnew Pasha, enveloped as a mark of peace in a \nvast white robe. He rode a fine Barbary steed",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
