      {
        "finding_id": "section-89fa4f9241c6ae392029",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "It’s interesting that Pére Dan mentions the terror roused \nin “our” European hearts by the music. The Janissaries \nappear to have been the very first in history to use military \nmarching music, and when their bands appeared blaring and \nbooming before the gates of Vienna, it’s said that Christian \nsoldiers threw down their weapons and fled at the mere \nsound. It would be interesting to know if the Ocak ever \nshipped a band aboard a corsair vessel (the Algerian \nJanissaries accompanied the pirates as men-at-arms, used \nonly when a prize ship was boarded and subdued by force). \nThe European pirates who operated in the Caribbean and \nIndian oceans in the 17—18th century are reputed to have \nbeen very fond of music, and to have hired on full-time pro- \nfessionals when they could afford to, but apparently the \nmusic was for their own pleasure rather than a form of psy- \nchological warfare!8",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
