      {
        "finding_id": "section-2e8460af63a32593cd0a",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "His most daring adventure, however, was \nto take a squadron of four galiots through the \nStraits to Salé, where he was joined by three \npirate captains, and then on to the Canaries. \nThe corsairs sacked Lanzarote, captured the \nwife and daughter of the governor and hun- \ndreds of people of lesser importance. After a \ncruise around the islands and several further \nlandings for more booty and prisoners, they \nhoisted a flag for parley and allowed the ran- \nsom of their more important captives. The rest \nwere carried back to Algiers or Salé as slaves. \nThe Spanish, forewarned of the corsairs’ \nreturn, tried to intercept them at the Straits, \nbut Morat Reis successfully evaded Don \nMartin de Padilla’s armada in a storm and \nbrought his little flotilla into Algiers. It was a \ndaring raid made more daring since the galiot \nwas not really a suitable vessel for the Atlantic. \nChristians liked to believe that God punished \nMorat Reis by causing his son to die just \nbefore his return, but the story, told in the tes- \ntimony about the raid made before the \nInquisition, may not be completely correct.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
