        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Another English renegado “gentleman” (from Cornwall) \nwas Ambrose Sayer [¢éi0., p. 83]. In 1613 Sayer was captain \nof an Algerian vessel which was captured at Salé by an \nEnglish ship, whose captain decided to send the corsairs \nback to London to stand trial. Toby Glanville, one of Sayer’s \nshipmates, realized the “game was up, made several attempts \nto commit suicide and eventually succeeded in throwing him- \nself off the stern of the ship.” [c40., p. 97] Presumably, like \nmost sailors, he'd never learned to swim. Captain Sayer was \nsent home and convicted of piracy, but somehow managed to \nescape —and presumably to retire, since we hear no more of \nhim.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1613
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
