      },
      {
        "finding_id": "section-de9aeb8241b9e8e67041",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "By 1605 Ward had succeeded in gathering a formidable \nforce around himself. His man-of-war, which he had appro- \npriately named the Gift, was a flyboat of 200 tons or more, \nmounting thirty-two guns and crewed by about 100 men. In \naddition to the Git, he was accompanied in his marauding by \nany prizes which he thought might suit his purpose. His men \nwere mainly English, but included a considerable number of \nDutchmen. There was certainly no shortage of able seamen \nwho were anxious to join his band. Ward’s pamphleteer, \nAndrew Barker, had an even higher estimate of the pirates’ \nabilities, saying that many of them were “worthy spirits, \nwhose resolutions, if they had beene aimed to honourable \nactions, either at sea or shore...might have beene preferred \nand commended for service to the greatest Prince living.”",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1605
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
