      {
        "finding_id": "section-771c8c326f334ca281a7",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Ward's protector at Tunis was Cara Osman, who, as \nhead of the Janissaries, had exercised absolute control over \nthe city since 1594. An agreement was reached between the \ntwo men whereby Osman had first refusal of all goods which \nthe pirates brought back to Tunis. The goods were then \nstored in Tunisian warehouses and resold to Christian mer- \nchants at a considerable profit. Everything points to the fact \nthat Ward and Osman enjoyed a good working relationship \nand they may have even become close friends, for the pirate \ncalled the Turk “brother”. The suspicion is, however, that \nOsman got the best of the bargain. Yet the pirates were utter- \nly dependent on Osman’s friendship, for without it they \nwould probably have been denied the use of Tunis as a base. \nThomas Mitton, a man who had lived at Tunis for three years \nand been to sea with Ward, testified to this when he gave evi- \ndence in the admiralty court:",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1594
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
