      {
        "finding_id": "section-e7e9ec8cc49c1880ed13",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The event that shook the Republic of Venice, and so \nenriched the pirates, was the loss of the Reniera e Soderina, a \n600-ton argosy. The great ship was taken as she lay becalmed \nnear Cyprus by two pirate ships commanded by Ward, each \nsaid to be mounting forty guns and carrying at least 100 \narmed men. Amongst the fabulous cargo of the Soderina was \nindigo, silk, cinnamon and cotton worth at least L100,000 \n(one wildly exaggerated English report put her value at “two \nmillions at the least”). It was not only the size of the financial \nloss which caused such a stir on the Rialto. The very manner \nof the Soderinas capture was a disgrace to the Republic of St. \nMark. From one account of the battle, it is clear that the \ncrew of the argosy were terrified by the ferocity of the \npirates’ attack and offered little or no resistance:",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
