        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "24. As Pére Dan describes it, day and night the noise of quarrels \narose from the taverns and Moorish cafés, most of them owned by \nindigenous merchants “to whom the pirates sold their booty”; the \ncorsairs at once spent their profits in “cabarets and other places of \ndebauch, since their greatest passion was to waste on revelry the \nwealth they’d won at sea.” [Coindreau, 1948: 41] Some feeling for \nthe “scene” might be gained from descriptions of Port Royal, the \nlater pirate town in Jamaica, which was so wicked that a flood \nswallowed it up like a watery Sodom. [Exquemelin, 1699]",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1699,
          1948
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
          "source_file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 121885,
          "end_char": 122473,
          "start_line": 3101,
          "end_line": 3109,
          "printed_page": null,
