      },
      {
        "finding_id": "section-cd9273f997ebc88dd1c0",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The new balance of power proved precarious, and in \n1631 al-Ayyashi broke the peace again. The Andalusians had \nbetrayed him by refusing to send him the scaling ladders he \nneeded in his seige of Mamora. He asked the religious lead- \ners of Old Salé for a fatwa or decision allowing him to repress \nthe corsairs of New Salé and the Casbah, “for they have \nopposed Allah and his Prophet and aided the infidels and \ngiven them counsel...they manage to their liking the proper- \nty of Muslims, depriving them of profit and monopolizing \ntrade to their benefit.” [Brown, 1971: 49] Al-Ayyashi opened \nfire with his cannons and launched a seige against the South \nbank which lasted till 1632 and then fizzled out in October \nof that year.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1631,
          1632,
          1971
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
