      {
        "finding_id": "section-6e51fa44d911e1d804ce",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "themselves—were not writers. All categories in which we \nmight discuss the corsairs have been predetermined by out- \nside hostility and propaganda. This is the fate of the revi- \nsionist historian attempting to investigate the culture —or the \npolitics of resistance —of a long-vanished non-literate com- \nmunity. Recently, of course, the revisionists themselves have \ndeveloped (or resurrected) some categories of their own. \nMarxist or Marxizing historians of “social banditry” and mil- \nlenialism, like Hobsbawm and Cohn, provide some useful \nmethodology, while writers of a more libertarian-leftist slant \n(like Hill, Lemisch, Linebaugh, and Rediker) have actually \ncreated a whole new historiography of maritime radicalism. \nBut none of them has discussed the Renegadoes. As far as I \nknow, no comparable school of thought has arisen amongst \nMoroccan or Algerian or Tunisian historians, who might have \naccess to untapped documentary resources (assuming such \nexist); orientalists have ignored the issue, whether out of \ntheir own innate cultural conservatism or because no texts \ncan be found; and so the field has been left to us amateur \npiratologists, faute de mieux.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
