      {
        "finding_id": "section-9af581ce4a25f225c58e",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Abmeod el-Cortobi, a Spanish renegade (or Morisco?) from \nCordoba, was a “fat man.” On October 6, 1658, commanding \nthe Saletine ship The Sun, he met with a Dutch fleet off Cape \nFinistere. Again Holland and Salé were supposed to be at \npeace, and Ahmed Rais decided to pay a friendly visit to the \nflagship. After returning to his own ship, he watched in hor- \nror as one of the Dutch vessels, Te Prophet Dantel of Lubeck \nunder Captain Pieter Noel, suddenly attacked him. Several \ncorsairs were killed, and the rest—including Ahmed —taken \nprisoner. The Dutchman then looted The Sun, set fire to her, \nand sank her. This singular event caused a great diplomatic \nscandal to erupt. Salé demanded recompense, and the Dutch \n(anxious to preserve the peace) took the affair quite serious- \nly. In January 1659 the Admiralty fined the captain of The \nProphet Daniel 9,500 florins, and handed over to Salé a vessel \nequal in tonnage and armament to the sunken Sw, while The \nProphet Daniel itself was awarded to Ahmed el-Cortobi. \n[Coindreau, 1948: 187]",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1658,
          1659,
          1948
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
