        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Later in the year an even greater force of \npirates, numbering eleven ships and 1,000 \nmen, assembled off the coast. [This was \nCaptain Ward and his fleet from Tunis.] Sir \nRichard Moryson, then the Vice-President of \nMunster, was powerless to take action against \nthem, and had to fall back on the old and obvi- \nously unsatisfactory method of pardoning \nthem. “The continual repair of the pirates to \nthe western coast of the province,” he told \nLord Salisbury, “in consequence of the \nremoteness of the place, the wildness of the \npeople, and their own strength and wealth, \nboth to command and entice relief, is very dif- \nficult for us to prevent or remedy.”",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
