      {
        "finding_id": "section-408f2d1832521528e8ae",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "groups of pirates formed their own social spheres, and that the \n“governments” of these groups (as expressed in ships’ “arti- \ncles”) were both anarchistic in affording maximum individual \nfreedoms, and communistic in eliminating economic hierar- \nchy. The social organization of the pirates has no parallel in \nany of the states of the 15—-18th centuries —except Rabat-Salé. \nThe Republic of Bou Regreg was not a pure pirate utopia, but \nit was a state founded on piratical principles; in fact, it was the \nonly state ever founded on these principles.30",
        "years_in_scope": [],
