      {
        "finding_id": "section-cba50afd72f844aaab62",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "ships needed to re-provision or repair, they might call in at any \nof several Moroccan coastal towns (at least during periods \nwhen these were not held by European powers) such as \nTetouan, Mamora, Fedala, Azemmour, or Safi. Sometimes \nsome of the fleet headed through the Straits of Gibraltar and \nraided the shipping and even the coasts of Mediterranean \nSpain and France —but this was usually considered the prop- \ner stomping grounds of Algiers, Tunis, and Tripoli. But the \nother Barbary state corsairs seldom if ever made it as far into \nthe Atlantic as the Sallee Rovers. In 1625 they carried off cap- \ntives from Plymouth in England; in 1626 five ships were seized \noff the coast of Wales; in 1627 they reached Iceland and \nsacked the city of Reykjavik, where the booty was scant but \nthe blond captives no doubt proved popular in the slave mar- \nkets. A great deal of activity centered in the waters between \nEngland and Ireland, and we assume that the corsairs used \nsome of the remote lawless smugglers’ ports of Southern and \nWestern Ireland as friendly harbours. In the Newfoundland \nbanks the Saletin fleet captured more than 40 fishing vessels \nin the space of two years, and in 1624 a dozen or so ships from \nSalé appeared on the coasts off Acadia or Nova Scotia. When \nthe English fleet came to Salé in 1637, the purpose was to ran- \nsom poor fishermen from English vessels seized off \nNewfoundland.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1624,
          1625,
          1626,
          1627,
          1637
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
