      {
        "finding_id": "section-0f6d9efcb4e3a08b4577",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Order, made up the spiritual practice added to the ordinary \nreligious practice of prayer, fasting, almsgiving, etc. The \ntremendous prestige of certain shaykhs could manifest itself \nas political power. It seems highly unlikely that any of these \nOrders would have appealed to or been willing to admit a \nRenegado, but it’s not impossible. In fact, a fascinating doc- \nument purporting to give an account of the captivity of St. \nVincent de Paul, hints that an educated captive and an edu- \ncated Moor might well share a fascination with mysticism. \nUnfortunately recent scholarship has cast this text into the \nrealm of pseudepigrapha; but luckily Gosse, in his stylish and \nentertaining History of the Pirates, still believed in the text’s \nauthenticity and quoted the whole thing. It may not be true, \nbut it’s extremely important because it was believed to be \ntrue—because it was believable. (This same argument will be \nmade for Defoe’s perhaps apocryphal account of Captain \nMission’s Libertatia.) Here is the relevant passage:",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
          "source_file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
          "start_char": 271118,
          "end_char": 272153,
          "start_line": 6907,
