      {
        "finding_id": "section-56618408e458feb34d6d",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "May we surmise that this autonomy meant something \nmore to the corsairs than merely a chance to maximize prof- \nits? In fact —was their brand of “perpetual revolution” really \ncompatible with any serious proto-capitalist designs and \nambitions? Wouldn’t a monarchy (preferably a corrupt \nmonarchy) have better served the purposes of simple fiscal \nagerandizement? Isn't there something guevatic about the \nwhole Bou Regreg phenomenon? With the possible excep- \ntions of the Venetian or Dutch Republics of oligarchs, and \nthe Zasffe of Algiers, the corsairs lacked any real-world mod- \nels for their democratic experiment.38 But the ea of a repub- \nlic was very much in the air—and by 1640 would emerge into \nEuropean history with the revolutions in England, then \nAmerica, then France. Was it just an accident of history that \nall this should be preceded by the Republic of Salé? Or should \nwe re-write the historical sequence to read: Salé, England, \nAmerica, France? An embarrassing thought, perhaps: \nMoorish pirates and renegade converts to Islam as the hid- \nden forefathers of Democracy. Better not pursue it.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1640
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
