      {
        "finding_id": "section-f2a3aa3f21d60c28011d",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The early Buccaneers lived a fairly idyllic life in the \nwoods, a life marked by extremes of poverty and plenty, cru- \nelty and generosity, and punctuated by desperate ventures to \nsea in leaky canoes and jury-rigged sloops. The Buccaneer \nway of life had an obvious appeal: interracial harmony, class \nsolidarity, freedom from government, adventure, and possi- \nble glory. Other endeavors sprang up. Belize was first settled \nby Buccaneers. The town of Port Royal on Jamaica became \ntheir stomping ground; its haunted ruins can still be seen \nbeneath the sea that drowned it whole in 1692. But even \nbefore this quietus of biblical proportions the Buccaneer life \nhad already come to an end. The brilliant Henry Morgan, \nbold and lucky, rose to leadership, organized the amazing \nBuccaneer invasion of Panama in 1671—then took the \nPardon along with an English appointment as Governor and \nHigh Judge, and returned to his old haunts as the execu- \ntioner of his old comrades. It was certainly the end of an era; \nthe surviving Buccaneers, cut adrift from permanent land \nbases, became pirates.",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1671,
          1692
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
