      {
        "finding_id": "section-b9452211c75e820ec62e",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The bo’sun Mathew le Tonder suggests flying the black \nflag (the so-called Jolly Roger) as their standard—but \nCaraccioli objects, saying “they were no pirates but men who \nwere resolved to effect the Liberty which God and Nature \ngave them.” He makes reference to “Peoples’ Rights and \nLiberties,” “shaking the yoak of tyranny,” the “misery of \noppression and poverty.” “Pirates were men of no principle \nand led dissolute lives; but their lives were to be brave, just, \nand innocent.” For their emblem they choose a white ensign \nwith the motto “For God and Liberty.” (All this sounds more \nlike Detwsm than “Atheism”, but in the early 18th century the \nterms were still virtually interchangable.)",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
