        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "allowed to push us into abandoning the solid historicity of a \nrevolutionary hero or a real utopia. (See, for example, the pref- \nace to Burroughs, 1981; also Law, 1980] Ranter’s Bay was \nreal enough, and so were the “Kingdoms” carved out in \nMadagascar by the “half-breed” children of the pirates. [See \nDeschamps, 1949, esp. pp. 215-229] The Buccaneers were \nreal, and so were the wild crew at Nassau in the Bahamas \n(including Blackbeard, and “Calico Jack” Rackham and his \ntwo pirate wives, Ann Bonney and Mary Reade), which \nflourished for a few years in the early 1700's. Libertatia could \nhave been real, and whould have been real; this much will suf- \nfice for the admirers of Capt. Mission. Christopher Hill, for \none, refuses to accept Mission as pure fiction. Hill points out \nthat although Defoe was a fire-breathing radical as a youth, \nhe had become a hack by the 1720's, and a supporter of bour- \ngeois property values. “This is what makes the fairness of his \ndescription of Libertatia so remarkable. This would be sur- \nprising if he had invented the whole thing, less so if he had \nbeen listening to old sailors’ tales and saw the possibility of \nusing Libertatia to criticize aspects of capitalist society which \noffended him.” [op. ctt., p. 179]",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1700,
          1720,
          1949,
          1980,
          1981
        ],
