      {
        "finding_id": "section-bc3641303e79afd9fa0d",
        "file": "pirate-utopias-hakim-bey_Pirate_Utopias_Bey_0_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Pirate Utopias: Moorish Corsairs & European Renegadoes",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/pirate-utopias-hakim-bey",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Hakim Bey"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1995-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "ebef0a63c86f8eca812a0716c8fde4bac770ce246530d69bc074d6640e161000",
        "evidence_excerpt": "History has tended to view the Renegadoes’ story as \nmeaningless, as a mere glitch in the smooth and inevitable \nprogress of European culture toward world domination. The \npirates were uneducated, poor, and marginalized —and \nhence (it is assumed) they could have had no real ideas or \nintentions. They are seen -as insignificant particles swept \naway from the mainstream of history by a freakish eddy or \nswirl of exotic irrationality. Thousands of conversions to the \nfaith of the Other mean nothing; centuries of resistance to \nEuropean-Christian hegemony mean nothing. Not one of the \ntexts I’ve read on the subject even mentioned the possibility \nof intentionality and resistance, much less the notion of a \n“Pirate Utopia”. The idea of the “positive shadow” of Islam is \nan ad hoc pro tem category I constructed in order to try to \nunderstand the enigma of apostasy; no historian (as far as I \nknow) has ever posited a connection between the intellectu- \nal Islamophilia of Rosicrucianism and the Enlightenment,",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
