      {
        "finding_id": "section-9529bc23d345ed39186f",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "the  veteran  Grand  Master  of  the  Knights  of  Saint \nJohn,  was  nominated  as  Captain-General  of  the \nChristian  armies.  For  the  purposes  of  this  war  the \nadmiral  of  the  Papal  galleys  in  the  Mediterranean, \nLodovico  del  Mosca,  purchased  from  Ferdinand, \nKing  of  Naples,  all  his  artillery,  of  which  a  descrip- \ntion is  given  by  the  Padre  Alberto  Guglielmotti,  a \nDominican  friar,  author  of  a  work  entitled,  \"  La \nGuerra  dei  Pirati  e  la  Marina  Pontifica  dal  1500 \nal  1560  A.D.\"  There  were  thirty-six  great  bom- \nbards, with  eighty  carts  pertaining  to  them ;  some \ndrawn  by  horses,  some  drawn  by  buffaloes  har- \nnessed singly,  or  two,  four,  or  even  six  together ; \ntwo  waggons  laden  with  arquebuses  for  ships'  boats  ; \nnine  with  about  forty  smaller  bombards  (bombardelles) \nplaced  three,  four,  or  even  six  on  each  waggon  ; \ntwelve  with  ordinary  pieces  of  artillery ;  as  many \nmore  for  the  service  of  twelve  big  guns  ;  thirty-seven \ncarts  of  iron  balls  ;  three  with  gunpowder ;  and \nfinally  five  laden  with  nitre,  darts,  and  bullets. \nSplendid  artillery  of  most  excellent  workmanship \nand  great  power  escorted  by  two  thousand  men \nunder  arms,  without  mentioning  the  companies \nwho  marched  before  and  after  each  waggon.\"",
        "years_in_scope": [
          1500,
          1560
        ],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
