      {
        "finding_id": "section-0c6ff142798904a07d04",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "It  was  late  autumn  when  the  expedition  at  last \nset  sail,  and  the  imperious  temper  of  Charles  was \nsuch  that  he  refused  to  be  governed  by  the  advice \nof  the  seasoned  mariners,  such  as  Andrea  and \nAntony  Doria,  and  others  who  dreaded  the  effect \nof  the  gales  which  the  armada  was  likely  to  encounter \non  the  coast  of  Africa.  The  Emperor  was  not  to  be \ngainsaid,  and  the  fleet  set  sail.  They  arrived,  says \nSandoval,  \"en  el  dia  de  San  Hieronymo,\"  Saint \nBartholomew's  day ;  and  there  then  arose  such \na  storm  as  the  Mediterranean  seldom,  sees.  Some \nof  the  army  had  landed,  some  were  still  afloat,  the \ncorsairs  accounted  for  the  luckless  soldiers  ashore, \nthe  elements  destroyed  many  left  in  the  ships : \n26  ships  and  4,000  men  were  lost.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
