      {
        "finding_id": "section-ede98badf322066f07a1",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Robeira  then  gave  the  order  to  fire,  and  at  the \nshort  range  into  packed  masses  of  men  the  volley \ndid  terrible  execution.  Completely  surprised,  the \ncorsairs  attempted  to  board,  but  were  repulsed  and \ndriven  back  with  more  slaughter.  His  men  be- \ncoming demoralised,  Hassan  withdrew  amidst  the \nferocious  taunts  of  the  Spaniards,  who  had  escaped \nalmost  unscathed.  Sore  and  angry,  the  corsairs \ncontinued  their  voyage  for  another  three  days,  at \nthe  expiration  of  which  they  arrived  at  Algiers. \nHassan,  who  had  acquired  quite  a  considerable \nbooty,  expected  a  warm  reception ;  this  he  re- \nceived, but  hardly  in  the  way  that  he  expected. \nHe  told  his  tale  to  Kheyr-ed-Din,  which  that \ncommander  received  in  frowning  silence ;  when \nhe  had  finished  the  storm  burst.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
