      {
        "finding_id": "section-63a4e042d9e7f3602303",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Barbarossa,  as  we  have  seen,  frankly  acknow- \nledged that  he  sought  his  own  advantage,  and, \nwhen  he  possessed  himself  of  Tunis,  made  no  pre- \ntence of  any  altruistic  motive.  The  Emperor,  on \nthe  other  hand,  having  come  in  the  guise  of  a \nChristian  reformer,  simply  stole  the  kingdom  from \nBarbarossa  and  kept  it  for  himself.  Incidentally \nhe  released  the  captives,  which  enabled  him  to  pose \nonce  more  as  the  great  champion  of  the  oppressed. \nBut,  however  this  may  have  been,  there  is  no  doubt \nthat  he  had  performed  a  notable  feat  of  arms,  and \neven  the  most  mighty  monarch  then  in  Europe  felt \nuplifted  by  the  fact  that  he  had  defeated  the  greatest \nof  the  corsairs  :  accordingly,  on  July  25th  Charles \nwrote  to  England,  France,  Portugal,  Milan,  Florence, \nVenice,  Genoa,  Siena,  Mantua,  and  Naples  :  \"  De \nmanera  que  en  pocas  dias  se  supo  in  toda  Europa  su \nbuena  fortuna.\"  (So  it  was  in  a  few  days  the  whole \nof  Europe  was  acquainted  with  his  good  fortune.)",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
