      {
        "finding_id": "section-de5b811b6496cb23321d",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "But  \"  the  veritable  man  of  the  sea  \"  was  in  some \nsort  to  console  him  for  that  which  he  had  lost ;  as \nnever  in  his  own  history — and  there  was  none  else \nwith  which  it  could  be  compared — had  the  Corsair \nKing  made  so  fruitful  a  raid.  He  ravaged  the \ncoasts  of  the  Adriatic  and  the  islands  of  the  Archi- \npelago, sweeping  in  slaves  by  the  thousand,  and \nby  the  end  of  the  year  he  had  collected  eighteen \nthousand  in  the  arsenal  at  Stamboul.  Great  was \nthe  jubilation  in  Constantinople  when  the  Admiral- \nissimo  himself  returned  from  his  last  expedition \nagainst  the  infidel ;  stilled  were  the  voices  which \nhinted  disaffection — who  among  them  all  could \nbring  back  four  hundred  thousand  pieces  of  gold  ? \nWhat  mariner  could  offer  to  the  Grand  Turk  such \nvaried  and  magnificent  presents  ?",
        "years_in_scope": [],
