      {
        "finding_id": "section-b217f0ee797200728bf0",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The  patience  of  the  veteran  gave  way  at  last ; \nnone  who  knew  Barbarossa  had  ever  seen  him \nshrink  from  fighting — to  this  his  whole  career  bore \nwitness.  He  had  delayed  the  issue  from  the  soundest \nof  strategical  reasons,  which  those  under  his  com- \nmand were  too  stupid  and  too  prejudiced  to  under- \nstand :  what  cared  they  for  reason  in  their  blind \nvalour  ? — they  wished  only  to  do  or  die  heedless \nof  the  fact  that  their  lives  might  be  spent  in  vain. \nTruly  it  was  no  thanks  to  the  subordinates  of  Kheyr- \ned-Din  that  this  campaign  did  not  end  in  disaster  to \nthe  arms  of  the  Ottoman  Porte.  Such  backing  as \nthe  admiral  had  came  from  among  his  own  men,  the \ncorsairs  whose  lives  had  been  spent  at  sea,  but  their \nopinions  were  but  dust  in  the  balance  once  the  all-",
