      {
        "finding_id": "section-762c77fdf9bf7f998c12",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "This,  the  most  historical,  if  not  the  greatest  feat \nin  the  life  of  Kheyr-ed-Din  Barbarossa,  was  for \nhim  a  triumph  indeed ;  with  a  vastly  inferior  force \nhe  had  driven  from  the  field  of  battle  his  \"  rival  in \nglory/'  as  he  himself  had  denominated  Andrea  Doria, \nand  he  had  accomplished  this  feat  notwithstanding \nthe  almost  mutinous  condition  of  his  own  forces. \nIn  spite  of  this  it  is  with  Condalmiero  and  with \nhim  alone  that  the  glory  of  this  day  must  rest ; \nalone,  absolutely  unsupported  as  we  have  seen,  he \nfought  one  of  those  fights  which  bring  the  heart \ninto  the  mouth  when  we  read  of  them  ;  the  stern \npride  of  the  Venetian  noble,  who  despised  as  canaille \nthe  pirate  hosts  by  whom  he  was  assailed,  had  its \ncounterpart  in  the  sturdy  valour  of  Chief  Bom- \nbardier Francisco  d'Arba  and  the  other  nameless \nheroes  of  which  that  good  company  was  composed ;",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
