      {
        "finding_id": "section-df1a0320e70642d2d544",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "La  Valette,  though  he  mourned  over  the  repulse \nof  the  Christian  forces  from  Tripoli,  did  not  on \nthat  account  allow  his  pursuit  of  the  infidel  to \ngrow  faint ;  the  galleys  of  \"  the  Religion  \"  were \nalways  at  sea,  and  both  the  corsairs  and  the  Otto- \nman Turks  were  perpetually  losing  valuable  ships \nand  costly  merchandise.  Under  the  General  of \nthe  Galleys,  the  Commandeur  Gozon  de  Melac, \nand  that  celebrated  chevalier,  the  Commandeur \nde  Romegas,  the  sea  forces  of  the  Knights  were \neverywhere  in  evidence.  Into  the  hands  of  the \nChristians  fell  thes  Peiion  de  Velez,  situated  on  the \nnorthern  coast  of  Africa  opposite  to  Malaga — a \nfortress  much  frequented  by  the  corsairs ;  the \nGoletta  at  Tunis  was  also  taken,  and  the  pirates \nbecame  so  much  alarmed  that  they  demanded \nsuccour  from  Constantinople.  They  represented  to \nSoliman  that,  at  this  rate,  the  whole  of  Northern \nAfrica  would  soon  be  in  the  hands  of  the  Christians \nto  the  total  exclusion  of  the  true  believer.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
