      {
        "finding_id": "section-73e38645d30f49ef8343",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Those  of  them  who  were  in  Algiers  demanded \n\"  Who  was  this  corsair  who  dared  to  keep  the \npicked  men  of  the  army  of  the  Grand  Turk  waiting \nfor  their  pay,  as  if  they  were  no  better  than  his \nslaves  ?  \"  Such  a  thing  as  a  mutiny  was,  in  the \ndays  of  which  we  speak,  a  matter  for  which  any \nprudent  corsair  had  to  be  prepared.  Ali  was  in  no \nmeans  discomposed,  and,  as  the  crisis  had  become \nacute  on  shore,  he  went  to  sea,  where  he  was  under \nno  obligation  to  pay  his  men,  who  paid  themselves \nat  the  expense  of  their  enemies.  He  put  to  sea  with \ntwenty  galleys,  and,  shortly  after  leaving  Algiers, \nhe  met  with  a  galley  from  the  Levant,  from  which \nhe  received  information  that  a  powerful  armada  was \npreparing  in  Constantinople  for  an  expedition  against \nthe  Christians.  He  steered  for  Coron^in  the  Morea, \nwhere  he  was  almost  immediately  joined  by  the \nOttoman  fleet,  the  commander  of  which  force  was \noverjoyed  to  find  so  formidable  a  reinforcement  under \nso  renowned  a  captain  as  Ali.",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "UNRESOLVED",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
