      {
        "finding_id": "section-74ae8f22a7dca0074496",
        "file": "seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_seawolvesofmedit00curruoft_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Sea-wolves of the Mediterranean : the grand period of the Moslem corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/seawolvesofmedit00curruoft",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": "Currey, Edward Hamilton",
          "date": [
            "1913-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": null,
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "8ded5d2596f6b8b5da24b9515a2ae1cca80e39c88e17cfd932f28761d003774e",
        "evidence_excerpt": "a  multitude  of  sins.  At  a  time  when  every  one \nwas  a  warrior  and  the  whole  world  was  an  armed \ncamp,  men  sought  great  captains  in  whose  following \nto  serve.  Among  the  Moslems  of  Northern  Africa, \nin  ordered  succession,  there  rose  to  the  surface  \"  veri- \ntable men  of  the  sea,\"  in  the  wake  of  whose  galleys \nravened  the  Sea-wolves.  When  we  consider  how \nundisciplined  and  how  stupidly  violent  these  pirates \nwere  by  nature,  and  how  they  were  welded  into  a \nhomogeneous  whole  by  those  of  whom  we  speak, \nwe  are  forced  to  the  conclusion  that  seldom,  in  all \nthe  ages,  have  abler  captains  arisen  to  take  fortune \nat  the  flood,  to  dominate  the  minds  and  the  bodies \nof  a  vast  host,  to  prove  that  they  were,  in  deed  and \nin  truth,  supreme  as  leaders  of  men.",
