        "source_text_sha256": "8d4d50eaf2b2a7a567e45f1745c54f1d7ef268dcc3cd5193895f31965f42ef69",
        "evidence_excerpt": "as  covering  the  Kalahari  The  ground  over \nlarge  areas  is  subject  to  an  annual  inundation \nfrom  the  network  of  rivers  flowing \ninto  the  lake  and  into  the  Zambesi. \nIn  the  drier  spots  the  grass  grows \nhigher  than  the  wagons  and \nfruits  festoon  the  trees.  The  banian  (F  • \nindica),  with  its  far-reaching  roots,  takes \nfirm,  hold  of  the  soil,  and  from  its  spreading \nbranches  gives  forth  aerial  roots,  which,  if  not \nnibbled  when  young  by  game,  descend  to  the \nsoil  and  taking  root  increase  the  area  shaded \nby  forming  subsidiary  trees.  On  other  ele- \nvated spots  the  camel  thorn  {Acacia  giraffe*.  . \nthe  white-thorned  mimosa  {Acacia  horrida\\ \nand  the  ubiquitous  baobab  flourish,  and \nwhere  sand  predominates  in  the  soil  the \ndate  and  Palmyra  palm  grow,  while  the \nmapani  is  still  to  be  seen.  All  along  the \nbanks  of  the  Chobe  (Kwando)  and  other  con- \nsiderable streams  enormous  reed  beds  ex- \ntending for  miles  give  cover  to  the  marsh- \nfrequenting  antelopes — the  leche  and  the \nmakong — and  where  the  streams  are  01 \nsufficient  depth  hippopotami  find  conge- \nnial quarters  and  exist  in  large  numbers. \nSerrated-edged  grass  cuts  the  hands  of  the \nincautious  grasper,  who,  stumbling  over  the \nthick,  binding  convolvulus,  has  to  use  his \nwhole  weight  to  bend  down  the  tangled  mass \nin  order  to  pass.  Fetid  gases  with  a  suspi- \ncious resemblance  to  sulphuretted  hydrogen \nrise  in  bubbles  from  the  disturbed  mud. \npoisoning  the  ah  almost  imprisoned  by  the \nhigh  reeds.  The  hot  strn  pours  down  with- \nout mercy  and.  bathed  in  perspiration,  the \nirritated,  wader  offers  an  irresistibly  tempting \nmeal  to  myriads  of  mosquitoes.  The  train ped- \ndown  \"  spoor;'  or  track,  of  the  hippopotamus \nmay  offer  a  way  partially  cleared,  but  his \nweight  has  caused  his  feet  to  leave  treach- \nerous holes,  into  which  the  biped  may  souse \nwithout  warning  and  find  himself  up  to  his \nneck  in  water  and  mud.  Ant-hills,  thirty \nfeet  high  and  many  yards  in  circumfer- \nence, rise  above  the  flooded  ground  and \nafford  the  only  secure  resting-places  and \neligible  sites  for  villa  residences,  of  which  the",
