      "title": "Corsairs of Algiers",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corsairs_of_Algiers",
      "identifier": null,
      "repository": null,
      "acquired_text_excerpt": "— Diego Haëdo, Histoire des rois d'Alger (History of the Kings of Algiers)200-year-old pirate flag at the Åland Maritime Museum originating from the North African coast\nIn the early 17th century, Algiers also became, along with other North African ports such as Tunis, one of the bases for Anglo-Turkish piracy. The peace in Europe forced the Norse privateers to shift their field of activity to the Mediterranean and to serve the enemies of Algiers. Yet many of those privateers converted to Islam and were enlisted in the Algerian corsair Navy. As a result of this privateer spill, international piracy activity in the region intensified to an unprecedented degree.[1] There were as many as 8,000 renegades in the city in 1634.[24][25]",
